Young's modulus, $Y=\frac{F L}{A \Delta L}=\frac{4 F L}{\pi D^{2} \Delta L}\Rightarrow \quad \Delta L=\frac{4 F L}{\pi D^{2} Y}$
The ratio of increase in length of steel and copper is $\frac{\Delta L_{s}}{\Delta L_{c}}=\frac{F_{s} L_{s}}{D_{s}^{2} Y_{s}} \times \frac{D_{c}^{2} Y_{c}}{F_{c} L_{c}}=\frac{F_{s}}{F_{c}} \times \frac{L_{s}}{L_{c}} \times\left(\frac{D_{c}}{D_{s}}\right)^{2} \times \frac{Y_{c}}{Y_{s}}$
Force on steel wire, $F_{s}=(2 m+5 m) g=7 m g$
Force on copper wire, $F_{c}=5 \mathrm{mg}$
\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow & \frac{F_{s}}{F_{c}}=\frac{7}{5} \\
\text { Also, } & \frac{D_{s}}{D_{c}}=p, \frac{L_{s}}{L_{c}}=q, \frac{Y_{s}}{Y_{c}}=s \\
\therefore \quad & \frac{\Delta L_{s}}{\Delta L_{c}}=\frac{7}{5} \times q \times \frac{1}{p^{2}} \times \frac{1}{s}=\frac{7 q}{5 s p^{2}}
\end{array}

